summary:
- topic: Student and Superintendent Reports
  text: Student Representative Eliot Wertheimer highlighted student achievements,
    including ArnaudSiohofor being named GatoradeMaineTrackandFieldAthleteof theYear,
    and various summer academic and recreational programs. Superintendent Matheney
    noted the fifth anniversary of the middle school referendum vote and introduced
    reports on AI and high school data.
- topic: Artificial Intelligence (AI) Initiative Update
  text: Director of Technology AndyWallace presented the Artificial IntelligenceSteeringCommittee's
    plan to adopt a controlled 'walled garden' approach using the Magic School AI
    tool. The strategy includes requiring staff competency training before using advanced
    AI, developing student curriculum on ethical AI use, and delaying formal policy
    changes in favor of adapting existing ones.
- topic: South Portland High School Data Review
  text: Principal SarahGlenn presented end-of-year data for SPHS, showing significant
    improvements. Chronic absenteeism dropped from 42.3% to 35.5%, out-of-school suspensions
    were reduced by 66%, and the course failure rate decreased from 6.7% to 4.4%,
    with a focus on restorative practices and student engagement contributing to the
    positive trends.
- topic: Facility Improvement Referendum Proposals
  text: 'Superintendent Matheney detailed three proposed bond referendums for the
    November ballot: $3 million for security vestibules and cameras at elementary
    schools, $1 million for deferred maintenance like roofing, and a $12.2 million
    project for the SPHS Stadium Complex. The stadium plan includes a new track, concessions,
    restrooms, and a synthetic turf field using IRONTURF and an organic BrockFILL
    infill.'
- topic: Public Comment on Stadium Project
  text: Numerous citizens, including parents and youth sports coaches, spoke overwhelmingly
    in favor of the stadium renovation and the synthetic turf field. Speakers cited
    the current field's poor condition, frequent cancellations due to weather, safety
    concerns like concussions, and the embarrassment of having substandard facilities
    compared to neighboring districts.
- topic: Board Action on Referendum Items
  text: The Board voted unanimously to send the $3 million security and $1 million
    maintenance bond questions to the City Council. After a lengthy debate and a contentious
    amendment to explicitly include 'synthetic turf fields' and adjust the total,
    the Board voted 6-1 to advance the stadium complex referendum as well, leaving
    the final decision on all three items to the City Council and, ultimately, the
    voters.
- topic: Committee Reports
  text: The FinanceCommittee reported that the FY24 budget is closing with a razor-thin
    margin, and celebrated a new $169,000 summer learning grant. The PolicyCommittee
    noted its ongoing work to review and rescind numerous outdated or redundant policies.
